G="Read-aloud pass"
E=[
(G,33,r'''Only Holy (the whole, complete, perfect) Truth can heal''',r'''Only Truth that is holy—whole, complete, perfect—can heal'''),
(G,35,r'''walked out of the tomb on Sunday, and the way back from a fractured world''',r'''walked out of the tomb on Sunday. The way back from a fractured world'''),
(G,37,r'''The goal is **communion**: the fruit of all four pillars together, truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice, the communion with God and neighbor we were made for.''',r'''The goal is **communion** with God and neighbor, the communion we were made for and the fruit of all four pillars together: truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice.'''),
(G,42,r'''Nowhere is this more clear than in understanding communion.''',r'''Nowhere is this clearer than in communion.'''),
(G,84,r'''something viscerally *personal*''',r'''something deeply *personal*'''),
(G,102,r'''**HOLY/WHOLE/COMPLETE perfection**''',r'''**HOLY, WHOLE, COMPLETE perfection**'''),
(G,104,r'''Perfect = living in full communion with God, neighbor, and true self.''',r'''To be perfect is to live in full communion with God, with our neighbor, and with our true selves.'''),
(G,114,r'''When God commands, "Say to all the congregation''',r'''When God tells Moses, "Say to all the congregation'''),
(G,116,r'''To "be holy" is to be "whole" in communion with God.''',r'''To "be holy" is to be "whole."'''),
(G,127,r'''when the mother first holds her newborn child.''',r'''when the mother first holds her newborn.'''),
(G,133,r'''nine months of total giving of her body, mind, and soul culminating in this moment.''',r'''nine months of giving her whole body, mind, and soul, culminating in this moment.'''),
(G,143,r'''When you're living what "You, therefore, must be perfect, as your heavenly Father is perfect" (Matthew 5:48) means: perfect, complete, holy communion.''',r'''When you're living out His command, "You, therefore, must be perfect, as your heavenly Father is perfect" (Matthew 5:48): perfect, complete, holy communion.'''),
(G,154,r'''What St. John Paul II called the culture of death, which our society has embraced—contraception, abortion, euthanasia, assisted suicide—treats life''',r'''Our society has embraced what St. John Paul II called the culture of death: contraception, abortion, euthanasia, assisted suicide. It treats life'''),
(G,167,r'''it binds everything else: heaven and earth—the eternal sacrifice of Christ becomes present in time; Christ and His Church—"He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood abides in me, and I in him" (John 6:56); believers to each other—"Because there is one bread, we who are many are one body, for we all partake of the one bread" (1 Corinthians 10:17); past, present, and future—the Last Supper, Calvary, and the eternal wedding feast all made present in one sacramental moment. This is the wedding feast of the Lamb foretold in Revelation: "And the angel said to me, 'Write this: Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb.' And he said to me, 'These are true words of God.'" (Revelation 19:9), where Christ and His Bride, the Church, are united in eternal communion.''',r'''it binds everything else. It binds heaven and earth: the eternal sacrifice of Christ becomes present in time. It binds Christ and His Church: "He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood abides in me, and I in him" (John 6:56). It binds believers to each other: "Because there is one bread, we who are many are one body, for we all partake of the one bread" (1 Corinthians 10:17). It binds past, present, and future: the Last Supper, Calvary, and the eternal wedding feast, all made present in one sacramental moment. This is the wedding feast of the Lamb foretold in Revelation, where Christ and His Bride, the Church, are united in eternal communion: "And the angel said to me, 'Write this: Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb.' And he said to me, 'These are true words of God.'" (Revelation 19:9).'''),
(G,171,r'''The One Bread—Christ's Body in the Eucharist—*constitutes* the One Body—the Church.''',r'''The One Bread, Christ's Body in the Eucharist, *constitutes* the One Body, the Church.'''),
(G,177,r'''Through study and humble submission to her wisdom, Sacred Tradition opens our eyes:''',r'''As we study and humbly submit to her wisdom, Sacred Tradition opens our eyes:'''),
(G,179,r'''The Eucharist IS the source and summit because it is both **SOURCE**—where we receive God's life, the sacred gift, communion with God as the starting point—and **SUMMIT**—where we achieve deepest communion, the goal, communion with God now and eternally as the end.''',r'''The Eucharist IS the source and summit because it is both. It is the **SOURCE**, where we receive God's life, the sacred gift, communion with God as our starting point. And it is the **SUMMIT**, where communion with God is deepest: the goal we were made for, now and for eternity.'''),
(G,181,r'''It mirrors our definition perfectly: **Sacred** (God's gift) leads to **Communion** (His Love poured out in us so that we pour out His love to others).  The Eucharist''',r'''It mirrors our definition perfectly: the **Sacred**, God's gift, leads to **Communion**, His love poured out in us so that we pour it out to others. The Eucharist'''),
(G,197,r'''Like Mary at the Annunciation—"And the angel said to her, 'The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you; therefore the child to be born will be called holy, the Son of God.'" (Luke 1:35)—we come to the altar empty-handed, receiving what we cannot give ourselves.''',r'''Like Mary at the Annunciation, we come to the altar to receive what we cannot give ourselves: "And the angel said to her, 'The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you; therefore the child to be born will be called holy, the Son of God.'" (Luke 1:35).'''),
(G,201,r'''So you rise and go to Him. In that physical act of processing toward the altar—like a bride approaching her bridegroom—bowing or kneeling in worship before Jesus, saying "Amen" to "the Body of Christ" or "the Blood of Christ," you give yourself to Him as He gives Himself to you.''',r'''So you rise and go to Him. You walk toward the altar like a bride approaching her bridegroom. You bow or kneel in worship before Jesus. You say "Amen" to "the Body of Christ" or "the Blood of Christ," and you give yourself to Him as He gives Himself to you.'''),
(G,219,r'''The Mass is the two-liturgy whole of His self-gift:''',r'''The Mass is His one self-gift in two parts:'''),
(G,233,r'''Christ Himself placed the condition of mercy at the very center of His Church's central prayer — spoken at the very threshold of Communion.''',r'''Christ Himself placed the condition of mercy at the heart of the prayer He taught us, and the Church prays it at the very threshold of Communion.'''),
(G,245,r'''And there is one more thing the Mass is—and it deserves far more than these few lines can give it, for it could fill a book of its own:''',r'''And the Mass is one thing more, which could fill a book of its own:'''),
(G,254,r'''reaches every generation intact—a *holy governing assembly* He founded''',r'''reaches every generation intact. She is a *holy governing assembly* He founded'''),
(G,256,r'''In a world where the bonds are dissolving—family, covenant, shared truth, the very communion that once held us together as a Christian people—the Church is called to be the rock that anchors a drowning world to God: a witness''',r'''The bonds are dissolving: family, covenant, shared truth, the very communion that once held us together as a Christian people. In such a world the Church is called to be the rock that anchors a drowning world to God, a witness'''),
(G,264,r'''the model of every *yes* every Christian is called to give''',r'''the model of the *yes* every Christian is called to give'''),
(G,268,r'''And the **Church Militant**, us on earth, are fighting''',r'''And the **Church Militant**, we on earth, are fighting'''),
(G,274,r'''the bond is not severed—we can still love them''',r'''the bond is not broken—we can still love them'''),
(G,318,r'''The path forward is humbly seeking the truth together—because truth is sacred, it comes from God—trusting that Christ will lead us to the unity He prayed for, working for communion while never compromising the truth that makes communion possible.''',r'''The path forward is to seek the truth together, humbly, because truth is sacred and comes from God. We trust that Christ will lead us to the unity He prayed for, and we work for communion without ever compromising the truth that makes communion possible.'''),
(G,327,r'''Will they see marriage as life-giving covenant or disposable contract? Will they see the Church as Body of Christ or social club?''',r'''Will they see marriage as a life-giving covenant or a disposable contract? Will they see the Church as the Body of Christ or a social club?'''),
(G,356,r'''Love is **sacred** (Ch 3), a **gift of covenant** (Ch 4) that **binds** (Ch 5). **Truth** (Ch 6) is the foundation—objective reality, Jesus IS truth. **Justice** (Ch 7) is giving what is due according to truth. **Mercy** (Ch 8) transcends justice without violating it. **Sacrifice** (Ch 9) is love made visible through action. And **Life-Giving Communion** (Ch 10) is''',r'''Love is **sacred** (Chapter 3), a **gift of covenant** (Chapter 4) that **binds** (Chapter 5). **Truth** (Chapter 6) is the foundation—objective reality, Jesus IS truth. **Justice** (Chapter 7) is giving what is due according to truth. **Mercy** (Chapter 8) transcends justice without violating it. **Sacrifice** (Chapter 9) is love made visible through action. And **Life-Giving Communion** (Chapter 10) is'''),
]
